WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.230
- [Instructor] The goal of this video

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:04.710
is to help us visualize what's
going on with the solution

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.730
at a microscopic really
at a molecular level.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.130
And also to get practice
drawing these types

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.300
of visualizations, because
you might be asked to do so,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.220
depending on the type of
chemistry class you're in.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.120
So what I have here are three
different aqueous solutions,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.900
which means that the solute
is dissolved in water.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.320
The first one is sodium chloride.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.140
Then we have magnesium chloride.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.623
Then we have C12-H22-O11,

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.220
which in other words is sucrose.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.640
And each of them are dissolved in water.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.880
What I'm gonna do is I'm gonna try to

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.060
do a drawing of what's
happening at a particulate level

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.150
in the respective
rectangles right below them.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:39.983
So, first of all,

00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:42.840
let's do think about what
happens with sodium chloride.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.720
So the first thing that you might realize,

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.960
is that sodium chloride,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.760
this is an ionic compound.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.670
It's made of up of a
sodium positively charged

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.360
ion or cation,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.593
and a chloride negatively charged ion.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.970
And if I wanna draw them,

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.540
and they're going to be
in a one to one ratio.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.740
For every sodium there's one chloride.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.830
And I could think about
their relative sizes

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.770
and to help us do so I'll
get out the periodic table

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.030
of elements, and we can
see here that they're both

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.020
in the third period.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.600
And if we were just looking at the atom,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.470
a sodium atom versus a chlorine atom,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.900
the general trend is
as you go to the right

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.880
and you have more electrons
in that outer most shell,

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.610
the radius tends to actually get smaller.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.870
So a chlorine atom is actually smaller

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.380
than a sodium atom but we're
not talking about atoms.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.120
We're talking about ions.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.420
So positively charged sodium
ion that has lost an electron.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.000
So it actually has an electron
configuration of neon,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.940
while the chloride anion has

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.200
an electron configuration of argon.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.445
So it actually turns out
that the chloride anion,

00:01:50.445 --> 00:01:54.190
is going to be larger
than the sodium cation.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:59.190
And so what I will do is I will represent

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.600
the chloride anion looking like this.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.390
I'll put a negative charge there,

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:10.020
and for the positively
charged sodium cation,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.510
I will make it a little bit smaller,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.530
something like that.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.720
And if I wanted to visualize
it for every sodium

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.790
positively charged ion.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:26.040
I would also have to
draw a chloride anion.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.150
So that's one of them.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.660
And then you might say,
"okay, well that's the solute,

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.760
but where is..."

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.810
And sometimes they actually
just ask you just to draw

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.950
the solute in which
case you would be done.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.040
But if you're wondering,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.890
well how is that
interacting with the water?

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.060
And this will even help us
understand what's happening.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.660
Why sodium chloride,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.880
why ionic compounds
dissolve well into water.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.240
Well then we have to
draw the water molecules.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.650
And if we still wanna get
the relative sizing right,

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.920
we can go back to our
periodic table of elements.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.760
And we know that the sodium cation,

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.000
has an electron configuration of neon,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.850
and oxygen is pretty cool close to that.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.310
And when we're talking about water,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.540
the oxygen atom is hogging the electrons.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.280
The electrons are spending
a little bit more time

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.610
around the oxygen than
around the hydrogens.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.530
So it's actually going
to be similar in size,

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:15.360
and oxygen and water and a sodium cation.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.940
And obviously these aren't
going to be exact drawings,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.360
but we can imagine each water

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.610
might look something like that.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.370
So it's an oxygen with two hydrogens.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.280
I'll just do it all in this
white color, right over here.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.610
And then the question is what
would their orientation be?

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.250
And that's really important to get right,

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.560
especially when you're dealing with water,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.770
which is a polar molecule.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.620
We know that the electrons spend more time

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:40.500
around the oxygen.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.490
We talked about this in many videos.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:45.570
So the oxygen end has a
partial negative charge,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.550
while the hydrogen ends have
a partial positive charge.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.840
And so the orientation of
a water molecule is that

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.400
the partially negative oxygen end,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.600
will be attracted to the positive ions.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.240
And then the positively
charged hydrogen ends

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.720
will be attracted to the negative ions.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.150
So you might have something like that.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.260
That's oxygen and then the two hydrogens.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.420
You might have an oxygen,

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.310
and then you have the two hydrogens.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.370
Because the hydrogens,

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:16.890
that end of the water molecule has

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.530
a partially positive charge.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.860
They're going to be attracted
to this chloride anion.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.230
You might have once again,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.730
the two hydrogens and then the oxygen.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:28.952
And once again, the oxygen
is going to be attracted

00:04:28.952 --> 00:04:32.780
to the sodium, the positively
charged sodium cations.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.600
And I could keep filling these in for,

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.730
in this entire space.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.440
But I think you get the
idea of what the water

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.590
would kind of look like and
how it would be oriented.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:43.860
Now for these next two,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.480
let's just focus on the solutes.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.093
What would the solute
look like in the solution?

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.420
Well, magnesium chloride,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.080
this once again is an ionic compound.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.080
And so it is going to disassociate
into its constituent ions

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.470
for every one magnesium ion,

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.980
actually going to have a plus two charge.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.320
You're going to have two,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.330
negatively charged chloride anions.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.180
And what's the relative sizing?

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.140
Well, to help us with that,

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.790
we go back to the periodic
table of elements.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.520
And if we're talking
about a magnesium two plus

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.900
or positively charged ion,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.070
it's still going to have an
electron configuration of neon,

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.500
and it's going to have more
protons than the sodium ion.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.440
So it's gonna pull even harder on them.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.900
So it's gonna be even
smaller than the sodium ion.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.650
So we could draw the
magnesium ions like this.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.180
Maybe I'll do two of them.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.480
So I'm gonna do it even
smaller than the sodium

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.910
and I'll write two plus,

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.960
because it has a positive two charge.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.090
I will write two plus again,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:47.630
'cause it has a positive charge.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:50.020
And for every one of those,

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.070
you're going to have two chloride anions.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:57.780
So maybe one there, maybe one there,

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.420
maybe one there and then maybe one there.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.850
And then if you were
asked to draw the solvent,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.660
draw the water, you would
orient it similarly.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.510
Where the partially
positive the hydrogen ends

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:14.270
of the water would be attracted to

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.800
the negative chloride anions.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.640
And the oxygen end of the water
molecules would be attracted

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:25.070
to these plus two charge magnesium ions.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:26.570
Now what about sucrose?

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.560
It isn't an ionic compounds.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.393
So in this situation,

00:06:29.393 --> 00:06:31.390
it's not going to disassociate.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:35.500
So a sucrose molecule
is relatively larger.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.660
I'll draw it like that.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:40.800
Maybe we have another one just like that.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.210
And the reason why it
dissolves in water well is,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.620
a sucrose molecule has
parts of the molecule

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.680
that have polarity to it.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.290
It has a lot of OH groups.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:52.470
So there's parts of the molecule
that are partially positive

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.760
and other parts of the molecule
that are partially negative,

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.560
other parts that are partially positive.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.420
And so that's able to be
attracted to the various ends

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.130
depending on whether
it's partially positive,

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:04.290
partially negative,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.480
of the water molecule.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.009
And so I'll just write it like this.

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:11.979
C12-H22-O11.

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:15.560
C12-H22-O11.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:16.393
For the sake of time,

00:07:16.393 --> 00:07:18.220
I haven't drawn the water molecules here

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.280
and to actually make, to
draw them intelligently,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.640
you would have to know what
parts of this larger molecule

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.460
have a partially positive
or partial negative charge.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.120
But as we'll see the
fact that you can either

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.790
disassociate into ions
that clearly have charge,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.480
or then you have a larger
molecule where parts of it

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.590
have a partial charge or have some charge

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:39.670
associated with it.

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:42.330
That's what allows it to dissolve well

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.953
into a polar solvent like water.

